{"title":"1990 USAMO Problems/Problem 2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A sequence of <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Function\" title=\"Function\">functions</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/9/59949c1d770092cabf7ba322885613f109a54d5c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\, \\{f_n(x) \\} \\,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"62\" height=\"18\" /> is defined <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Recursion\" title=\"Recursion\">recursively</a> as follows:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/9/f99ce5a5223238b6e4c778b5e0c38441abd3c49d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} f_1(x) &amp;= \\sqrt {x^2 + 48}, \\quad \\text{and} \\\\ f_{n + 1}(x) &amp;= \\sqrt {x^2 + 6f_n(x)} \\quad \\text{for } n \\geq 1. \\end{align*}\" width=\"282\" height=\"62\" />\n(Recall that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/f/e5f47dc6b447e054a3d1806dfb40f12d3932582f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sqrt {\\makebox[5mm]{}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"39\" height=\"18\" /> is understood to represent the positive <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Square_root\" title=\"Square root\">square root</a>.) For each positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, find all real solutions of the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/3/d73811955191480958a85b6491e00828c91183ce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\, f_n(x) = 2x \\,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"88\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We define <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/5/885d26274ba8af8233a80586cbb46903dc6afca3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_0(x) = 8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"73\" height=\"18\" />.  Then the recursive relation holds for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/3/02349576cee613512cdf301a365f06c0760acab5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, as well.\n</p><p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/3/7233b172cbd1a8d72b8a6383cb3d2eb1540e7807.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_n (x) \\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"18\" /> for all nonnegative integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, it suffices to consider nonnegative values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>We claim that the following set of relations hold true for all natural numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and nonnegative reals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/b/d8bd8edd9250073987b215495c73a219716b3005.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} f_n(x) &amp;&lt; 2x \\text{ if }x&gt;4 ; \\\\ f_n(x) &amp;= 2x \\text{ if }x=4 ; \\\\ f_n(x) &amp;&gt; 2x \\text{ if }x&lt;4 . \\end{align*}\" width=\"154\" height=\"68\" />\nTo prove this claim, we induct on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.  The statement evidently holds for our base case, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/3/02349576cee613512cdf301a365f06c0760acab5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p>Now, suppose the claim holds for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.  Then\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/0/5505749b4755541931635953af52237641baf3fb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*} f_{n+1}(x) &amp;= \\sqrt{x^2 + 6f_n(x)} &lt; \\sqrt{x^2+12x} &lt; \\sqrt{4x^2} = 2x, \\text{ if } x&gt;4 ; \\\\ f_{n+1}(x) &amp;= \\sqrt{x^2 + 6f_n(x)} = \\sqrt{x^2 + 12x} = \\sqrt{4x^2} = 2x, \\text{ if } x=4 ; \\\\ f_{n+1}(x) &amp;= \\sqrt{x^2 + 6f_n(x)} &gt; \\sqrt{x^2+12x} &gt; \\sqrt{4x^2} = 2x, \\text{ if } x&lt;4 . \\end{align*}\" width=\"487\" height=\"111\" />\nThe claim therefore holds by induction.  It then follows that for all nonnegative integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/7/2e7d65cfa316d639c9267398009812f3eb45fea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> is the unique solution to the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/7/db7b2b3d437e4b76a46f1a0b0c7c4a58f52c84a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_n(x) = 2x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"85\" height=\"18\" />.  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/b/3bbb9e25e5be095e317771a9c5d00877ee9c8012.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\blacksquare$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We claim that the only solution is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/a/c/6acf3c232bcbfa92ec01019ad9ce33244ec8176d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{x=4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"57\" height=\"25\" /> for all such <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. To show this, we consider all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and find solutions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>Before we consider solutions, we show that for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/e/05e948591bafa89c6a32fafbadb794873bdefd23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_n(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"18\" /> is positive for all positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> by induction. For our base case:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/9/609b0e3675ecc42b0fe452712075804f89ee64b3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f_1(x)=\\sqrt{x^2+48}\\ge\\sqrt{48}&gt;0\\]\" width=\"228\" height=\"21\" />\nso it is positive. Next, for our inductive step, assume for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/0/bf08f4d4d7f8dc8631a30576240cdda9db56f889.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=k-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"75\" height=\"13\" /> that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/5/2d543cd6a013a2d4432c35788ffa9e1ad2a8a67b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_{k-1}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"60\" height=\"20\" /> is positive; thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/6/be6c4f52aa506875a623fd09a69c2ac13957a8fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_{k-1}(x)&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"94\" height=\"20\" />, and we show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/d/5adb41b6d0d7dec9195b2ce3dd1aa799dfcd0636.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_k(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"43\" height=\"20\" /> is positive:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/8/be8ad9edce8ba821356b29ee7f6157c663611715.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f_k(x)=\\sqrt{x^2+6f_{k-1}(x)}\\ge\\sqrt{0+6f_{k-1}(x)}&gt;\\sqrt{0+0}=0\\]\" width=\"444\" height=\"32\" />\nthus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/e/0ce74be836859c975363a281d0f3d63d90cb3dd0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_k(x)&gt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"77\" height=\"20\" />, so we have proven the claim by induction.\n</p><p>First, we consider negative <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />. We know that for negative <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, if the equation were to have a solution, we would have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/9/b/99b96f601fb2b5560baedb8055e26009b2b69e83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_n(x)=2x&lt;0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"121\" height=\"20\" /> for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />. However, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/e/05e948591bafa89c6a32fafbadb794873bdefd23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_n(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"18\" /> is always nonnegative since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/e/05e948591bafa89c6a32fafbadb794873bdefd23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_n(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"18\" /> is always the square root of some number, which can never be negative; thus there are no solutions in this case.\n</p><p>Next, we consider <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/e/34ea5b6f6de584d56d19a89cc4923a9d9a5cfa41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=0$\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />. Solutions would have to satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/6/6d68b0ff91e26a27af43da5be75be21e2d15d405.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_n(0)=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"76\" height=\"20\" />; we have previously shown that all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/e/05e948591bafa89c6a32fafbadb794873bdefd23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_n(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"18\" /> are positive, so there are no such <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>Finally, we consider positive <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />. We divide this set into three groups: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/4/d44126f44116d38df1dc527564fb26da9a89b25d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&lt;4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/7/2e7d65cfa316d639c9267398009812f3eb45fea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/2/e12fb3c8310f1aab1f5e100c9f15efd364dbdad0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&gt;4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />. We claim that for increasing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/e/05e948591bafa89c6a32fafbadb794873bdefd23.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_n(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"18\" /> is decreasing, constant, and increasing, respectively, and we prove this using induction. First, we analyze <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/4/d44126f44116d38df1dc527564fb26da9a89b25d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&lt;4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />. Then\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/c/3cceda200076dfbe7919a3ce7bde439f30536da3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f_1(x)&lt;\\sqrt{4^2+48}=\\sqrt{64}=8\\]\" width=\"227\" height=\"21\" />\nFor our base case:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/e/15eb55bb2c3d28f5dfc1f74b56beb5ada2fe61fa.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f_2(x)=\\sqrt{x^2+6f_1(x)}&lt;\\sqrt{x^2+6\\cdot8}=f_1(x)\\]\" width=\"347\" height=\"32\" />\nThus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/6/9365e084115eaaebec32298e5fb9484e8851f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_2(x)&lt;f_1(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"108\" height=\"20\" />. For our inductive step, if for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/0/bf08f4d4d7f8dc8631a30576240cdda9db56f889.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=k-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"75\" height=\"13\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/4/5/b454f0a65d6c6b5b7e1d26820e5a1f9e6f90b3af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_k(x)&lt;f_{k-1}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"126\" height=\"20\" />, we show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/3/5b3d714f91ef4656c90e51f4b2d3ceea7a64c13f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_{k+1}(x)&lt;f_k(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"126\" height=\"20\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/9/1a997bc835c175436a58c27967e6e726010a90dd.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f_{k+1}(x)=\\sqrt{x^2+6f_k(x)}&lt;\\sqrt{x^2+6f_{k-1}(x)}=f_k(x)\\]\" width=\"405\" height=\"32\" />\nThus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/c/58ca535b888bf7116de5a668262b641b56178813.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_1(x)&gt;f_2(x)&gt;f_3(x)&gt;\\ldots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"218\" height=\"20\" /> and the conclusion follows.\n</p><p>Next, for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/7/2e7d65cfa316d639c9267398009812f3eb45fea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/6/7960a95b56c533755895a2b75f41b94fb8e0972c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_1(4)=8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"75\" height=\"20\" /> by substituting. Then, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/6/f269d8f4c1dd3862f60027aff75e837ffda47bbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_{n-1}(4)=8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"93\" height=\"20\" />, we show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/8/1082c25ff64b096436981f979cab07533fd5263f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_n(4)=8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"76\" height=\"20\" /> as well:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/f/caf21d05507458db60eb83c24317c4362084212e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_n(4)=\\sqrt{x^2+6f_{n-1}(x)}=\\sqrt{16+6\\cdot8}=\\sqrt{64}=8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"396\" height=\"35\" />\nso the sequence is constant. Notice that in this case, the equation we must solve becomes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/8/1082c25ff64b096436981f979cab07533fd5263f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_n(4)=8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"76\" height=\"20\" />, which is true for all positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.\n</p><p>Finally, we analyze <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/2/e12fb3c8310f1aab1f5e100c9f15efd364dbdad0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&gt;4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />. Then\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/4/53472b7df77eff1edddcfb0c02b4bb5da3ed870d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f_1(x)&gt;\\sqrt{4^2+48}=\\sqrt{64}=8\\]\" width=\"227\" height=\"21\" />\nFor our base case:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/5/f4558e2ba4d602a571f0d0f99d9be5b621c5ae58.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f_2(x)=\\sqrt{x^2+6f_1(x)}&gt;\\sqrt{x^2+6\\cdot8}=f_1(x)\\]\" width=\"347\" height=\"32\" />\nThus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/f/f9ffeca54425fe5eed0e57cd68414ab72b8c64bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_2(x)&gt;f_1(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"108\" height=\"20\" />. For our inductive step, if for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/0/bf08f4d4d7f8dc8631a30576240cdda9db56f889.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=k-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"75\" height=\"13\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/8/248dc871a996fadae324eff161e73163692241e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_k(x)&gt;f_{k-1}(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"126\" height=\"20\" />, we show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/b/12b60f54f37a14bb11a6af704d053c184b3a37b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_{k+1}(x)&gt;f_k(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"126\" height=\"20\" />:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/b/a9b13ba8e0f8f69fe3afa2002552ffa6d6a012ed.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f_{k+1}(x)=\\sqrt{x^2+6f_k(x)}&gt;\\sqrt{x^2+6f_{k-1}(x)}=f_k(x)\\]\" width=\"405\" height=\"32\" />\nThus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/3/723a1266b8a7fe7be13d2e835e1ae0c6e2d90515.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_1(x)&lt;f_2(x)&lt;f_3(x)&lt;\\ldots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"218\" height=\"20\" /> and the conclusion follows.\n</p><p>If for some positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/e/61ed07610a1b8d000c2f6055d6fb31eb2ea70247.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_n(x)=2x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"88\" height=\"20\" />, then:\n</p><p>\\begin{align*}\nf_n(x)&amp;=\\sqrt{x^2+6f_{n-1}(x)}\\\\\n2x&amp;=\\sqrt{x^2+6f_{n-1}(x)}\\\\\n4x^2&amp;=x^2+6f_{n-1}(x)\\\\\n3x^2&amp;=6f_{n-1}(x)\\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}x^2&amp;=f_{n-1}(x)\n\\end{align*}\nHowever, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/7/247848467a08806dce31c16dcec4d40b7ae24a0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;x&lt;4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"79\" height=\"15\" /> is a solution, then we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/2/592c897b1bbbe711da3fc362d629326fa12238a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_{n-1}(x)&gt;f_n(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"128\" height=\"20\" />; substituting values yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/8/b68a4f13c95fcc6f4c77e7880dbc63ed51bf8724.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}x^2&gt;2x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"76\" height=\"38\" />, which implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/2/e12fb3c8310f1aab1f5e100c9f15efd364dbdad0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&gt;4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />; this is a contradiction. Similarly, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/2/e12fb3c8310f1aab1f5e100c9f15efd364dbdad0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&gt;4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" /> is a solution, then we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/9/459cc66a1f048685fbce7cb21407c0cac400bd1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f_{n-1}(x)&lt;f_n(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"128\" height=\"20\" />; substituting values yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/4/c94843811c5373aebfa14f38b36ed7d6899573e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}x^2&lt;2x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"76\" height=\"38\" />, which implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/4/d44126f44116d38df1dc527564fb26da9a89b25d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&lt;4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />; this is also a contradiction.\n</p><p>As a result, since we have already established that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/7/2e7d65cfa316d639c9267398009812f3eb45fea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> is a solution for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and no other <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> can be solutions, we conclude that for each positive integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, the only real solution of the equation is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/7/2e7d65cfa316d639c9267398009812f3eb45fea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>~ <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:Eevee9406\">eevee9406</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_USAMO_Problems/Problem_2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:40%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_USAMO\" title=\"1990 USAMO\">1990 USAMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_USAMO_Problems\" title=\"1990 USAMO Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=27&amp;year=1990\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1990 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">Problem 1</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1990 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">Problem 3</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_USAMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1990 USAMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_USAMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1990 USAMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_USAMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1990 USAMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1990_USAMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1990 USAMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=USAMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"USAMO Problems and Solutions\"> All USAMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<ul><li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?p=356624#p356624\">Discussion on AoPS/MathLinks</a></li></ul>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017182314\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.053 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.072 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 503/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 1267/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 44/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 16908/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   13.628      1 -total\n 57.62%    7.852      1 Template:USAMO_box\n 38.43%    5.237      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:5577:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017182314 and revision id 245151. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}